[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Performance Optimization in LazyLists and Rendering"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jetpack Compose relies on an efficient rendering pipeline to display large collections using LazyColumn and LazyRow. However, inefficient state reads, unoptimized items, and frequent recompositions can cause frame drops and scroll jitter. This tutorial covers practical techniques to keep your Compose lists smooth and responsive."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Provide Stable Keys in LazyLists"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, LazyLists identify items using their position in the list. When an item is inserted, removed, or reordered, position-based keys force Compose to recompose unaffected items. Providing explicit, unique keys allows Compose to retain state and skip recomposition across list changes."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"LazyColumn {
    items(
        items = listItems,
        key = { item -> item.id }
    ) { item ->
        ListItemRow(item = item)
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Utilize contentType for Layout Recycling"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When displaying lists with heterogeneous items (e.g., headers, ads, and list items), specify 'contentType'. Compose uses this information to re-use compositions across items of the same type, similar to view types in RecyclerView."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"LazyColumn {
    items(
        items = feedList,
        key = { item -> item.id },
        contentType = { item -> item.type }
    ) { item ->
        FeedRow(item = item)
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Optimize Scroll State Reads with derivedStateOf"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reading high-frequency state changes directly—such as 'LazyListState.firstVisibleItemIndex'—during composition triggers recomposition on every pixel scrolled. Wrap these reads in 'derivedStateOf' so recomposition only happens when the final boolean or derived condition actually changes."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"val listState = rememberLazyListState()
val showScrollToTopButton by remember {
    derivedStateOf { listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0 }
}

if (showScrollToTopButton) {
    ScrollToTopButton(onClick = { /* Scroll to top logic */ })
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Defer State Reads to Layout or Draw Phases"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"If state changes only affect properties like rotation, translation, or opacity, avoid reading state directly inside the composition phase. Use lambda-based modifiers such as 'Modifier.graphicsLayer' to defer state reading to the draw phase, bypassing composition entirely."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Deferring scroll-based opacity changes to the draw phase
Modifier.graphicsLayer {
    alpha = if (listState.isScrollInProgress) 0.5f else 1.0f
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Ensure Parameter Stability"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Compose skips recomposing a list item only if all its parameters are stable. Classes with mutable fields, untyped collections (like List<T> from standard interfaces), or types from external modules without explicit stability annotations may be treated as unstable."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Annotate custom data classes with @Immutable or @Stable to enforce skipping behavior."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use kotlinx.collections.immutable types (e.g., ImmutableList) for reliable collection stability."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pass lambda callbacks using remember or method references to avoid lambda instance generation during recomposition."}]
